  tutorgrid -- does a FOUR-SIGNAL conditional code cross the air? ctxgrid
  names four on both axes with a HOST index; tutorwide transmits TWO with
  the pupil's own derived index. Nobody has run the two together.
  the code      kGridWords, the 2x2 ctxgrid established -- two F1 levels x
                two F2 levels, every target inside the MEASURED learned
                reach. Two BITS on two axes, not four levels on one:
                ctxfour read four levels on ONE axis at chance.
  the arms      g0-oracle (ctx_src 0) and g0-self (ctx_src 4) separate the
                two unknowns, so a failure says WHICH failed; then `wide`
                and `flip` carry the mapping with the width held.
  the bars      g0-self clears 3 SE on BOTH bits (prerequisite); `wide`
                clears 3 SE on both at generation 2 (primary); `flip`
                ALTERNATES SIGN between generations (inheritance, and
                without it a positive primary is VOID -- the tape
                re-imposes a width whether or not anything is inherited).
